---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import WorkshopVideo from '../components/WorkshopVideo.astro'
import { ogPathFor } from '../data/og'
import { labelFor, entry } from '../data/readiness.mjs'

// housesitter keeps an always-on agent Mac alive through a small IP-KVM. Every claim here is sourced from
// ourostack/housesitter: its README (the story, what's verified, the password rules), its real `housesitter doctor`
// run and `housesitter demo`, and the KVM's own recording of a real restart.
const title = 'housesitter: your agents’ Mac, unlocked after a restart | Ouro'
const description = 'A $100 KVM beside your agents’ Mac. When the Mac restarts, it unlocks FileVault. When it can’t, it tells you. On a real Mac mini, housesitter went from Restart to desktop in 70 seconds with nobody touching it.'
const link = 'text-scale underline underline-offset-4 decoration-scale/40 hover:text-glow hover:decoration-current hover:decoration-2 transition-colors'
const status = labelFor('housesitter')
const statusDetail = entry('housesitter').detail
const repo = 'https://github.com/ourostack/housesitter'
const demo = 'npx github:ourostack/housesitter demo'

// From the sitter's own log and the KVM's frames, the restart in the recording (docs/proof/2026-11-09-v0.2 in the repo).
const restart = [
  ['22:55:55', 'Restart, pressed by our agent: the only touch.'],
  ['22:56:01', 'The Mac shuts down.'],
  ['22:56:24', 'The screen went dark, then the Mac found the KVM’s keyboard again: a boot.'],
  ['22:56:44', 'FileVault, the prompt it was taught on the restart before, steady for six looks: now it may type.'],
  ['22:56:51', 'Typed the password, once, by itself.'],
  ['22:57:06', 'The desktop, 70 seconds after Restart.'],
]

const does = [
  ['Unlocks FileVault after a restart', 'Types the password at the FileVault screen you taught it, and only there, once you’ve armed and verified it; three times an hour at most. Your lock screen stays yours.'],
  ['Power-cycles a frozen Mac', 'Built, not yet tried on a real plug. With a smart plug on its cord, only a desktop frozen for 5 minutes that nothing answers for, confirmed by three more looks. Never at a boot or a login.'],
  ['Tells you only when it needs you', 'One message when it’s stuck, saying what to fix, a reminder every six hours at most, and “back” when the Mac is.'],
  ['Says so if the KVM itself dies', 'A dead KVM can’t message you, so it pings a dead man’s switch you choose every minute, and that tells you when the pings stop.'],
]

const whyNot = [
  ['A smart plug?', 'It restarts the Mac straight into the FileVault screen, where it waits for you.'],
  ['A PiKVM, or any KVM?', 'You can type the password from your phone, once you notice, if you’re awake. housesitter is that KVM, with nobody needing to notice.'],
  ['macOS 26’s SSH unlock?', 'Use it if you can. It needs Remote Login on and the network up before boot, and it can’t help a Mac whose login window crashed.'],
]

const cables = [
  ['HDMI', 'from the Mac’s own port into the KVM, so it sees the screen.'],
  ['USB-C', 'from the KVM into a port on the Mac itself, so it can type at the FileVault screen.'],
  ['Ethernet', 'for the KVM, so you reach it over your tailnet or LAN.'],
]

const safe = [
  'It types only where the password prompt is on the KVM’s own picture: read with OCR, or matched against the prompt you taught it from three frames at the FileVault screen (on our mini, the avatar and name just above the field). That lesson matches every FileVault frame of our restart and never the lock screen or a bare wallpaper. A desktop is a hard no, and so is a prompt on another display.',
  'Agents click, press keys and type only on a desktop, read fresh from the KVM’s picture. At a password screen, only the sitter types.',
  'Typing ships disarmed. A stored password is unverified until one supervised try reaches the desktop; a new password starts over, and a desktop appearing on its own never counts.',
  'The password lives in two places: your Keychain and the KVM, sealed and root-only. Never in argv, a log, a gist or a cloud. Nothing types it remotely.',
  'Whoever has root on the KVM, or the Mac and the KVM stolen together, has the password. Keep the KVM where the Mac is, on a network you trust.',
  'It never changes the KVM’s EDID or the Mac’s display mode. Doing that once crashed our mini’s login window.',
  'On your tailnet the KVM is tag:kvm and reaches nothing. GL.iNet’s cloud binding is off, and read back as off.',
]

const verified = [
  ['Done on a real Mac', 'FileVault after a restart, hands-off, by housesitter 0.2: Mac mini (M5 Pro) and GL.iNet Comet, 70 seconds from Restart to desktop. In the KVM’s own access log of those minutes, the only key and click events are the Restart clicks and the sitter’s three keys; the rest are video sessions, whose contents kvmd doesn’t log, and the log we publish is an extract (see the proof). Earlier that night, on 0.1, we taught it the FileVault screen once and proved its password, then it did the same hands-off in 79 seconds.'],
  ['Run on real hardware', 'housesitter doctor, look and status, read-only, against that Comet. It found a real bug, now fixed with a test.'],
  ['Done on a real Mac, too', 'When an agent needs the UI, if you allow it: housesitter unlocked the lock screen in 9 seconds, and the login window’s list of users in 16, clicking the account’s tile first.'],
  ['Sent for real', 'One TEST alert, end to end: the sitter’s own rules decided a Mac stuck at FileVault and sent that exact message, marked TEST. With --email it reached our HEY Imbox 2 seconds later.'],
  ['Next', 'The smart plug, which arrives 9 November.'],
  ['Not yet', 'A real freeze and a power cut; a message from a real refusal (none has happened yet); a macOS update; OCR beyond English macOS.'],
]
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} ogImage={ogPathFor('/housesitter')} canonical="https://ouroboros.bot/housesitter/">
  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-16 pt-32 pb-[2.8125rem] md:pb-10 md:pt-44">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto grid gap-10 xl:grid-cols-[1fr_1.15fr] product-hero">
      <div class="grid gap-5 content-start min-w-0 hero-copy">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2">
          <h1 class="heading-lead">housesitter</h1>
          <span class={`tag ${status.tone}`}>{status.label}</span>
        </div>
        <p class="product-lede" data-hero-lede>A $100 KVM beside your agents’ Mac. When the Mac restarts, it unlocks FileVault. When it can’t, it tells you.</p>
        <div class="grid gap-2" data-primary-action>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
            <pre id="housesitter-demo" class="command">{demo}</pre>
            <button type="button" data-copy-target="housesitter-demo" class="btn-ghost !py-2 text-sm"><span data-copy-label>Copy</span></button>
          </div>
          <span class="caption-mono">Real frames from that day. No hardware needed.</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-lg text-mist leading-relaxed">We pressed Restart on our Mac mini and touched nothing else. The KVM saw FileVault through the Mac’s own HDMI port, recognised the prompt it had been taught on the restart before, and typed once, by itself: 70 seconds from Restart to desktop.</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-mist">{statusDetail}: <a href={`${repo}/issues/11`} class={link}>follow it</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="min-w-0 hero-media restart-clip">
        <WorkshopVideo src="/images/workshop/housesitter-restart.mp4" poster="/images/workshop/housesitter-restart-poster.webp" width={960} height={604} alt="The KVM's own picture of our Mac mini during a restart, with a caption under each frame: logging out 3 seconds after Restart, which our agent pressed, the only touch; shutting down; boot seen, the Mac found the KVM's keyboard again; FileVault, the screen it was taught, steady, now it may type, the prompt zoomed twice; typed once, by itself, the field blurred; logging in; then a card: 70 s from Restart to desktop, nobody at the Mac, housesitter 0.2 on the KVM beside it." />
        <figcaption class="night-caption"><span class="caption-note">Real run</span> <span>Unlocked by housesitter 0.2 at 22:56 on 8 November. Only the shutdown, boot and FileVault frames are shown, and the typed field is blurred, because its dots would give away the password’s length.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-16 py-12 md:py-16 border-t border-canopy/20 bg-deep/40" aria-labelledby="restart-heading">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto">
      <h2 id="restart-heading" class="heading-section">From the sitter’s log <span class="text-scale">and the KVM’s frames</span></h2>
      <ol class="restart-log mt-8" aria-label="From the sitter's log and the KVM's frames">
        {restart.map(([at, what]) => (<li><span class="restart-at">{at}</span><span>{what}</span></li>))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-16 py-12 md:py-16 border-t border-canopy/20" aria-labelledby="why-built-heading">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto prose-col">
      <h2 id="why-built-heading" class="heading-section">Why I <span class="text-scale">built it</span></h2>
      <p class="story">If your agents live on a Mac with FileVault, every restart (an update, a power blip, a panic) stops at the password screen until someone types. A smart plug can’t type. A KVM can, if you’re awake. Here’s how we found out.</p>
      <p class="story">On Sunday morning one of our agents changed a display setting on the KVM beside our Mac mini. Ten seconds later macOS’s login window and its sign-in services crashed, and every agent on the mini went down with them. We typed the right password through the KVM three times, and none of it reached a password field.</p>
      <p class="story">Our first key was Escape. At a macOS login window, Escape switches to the list of users, where no field has focus. And the display the KVM was watching didn’t show the field at all. The mini sat locked from 10:11 until I logged in by hand at 17:54.</p>
      <p class="story">So housesitter types only where it sees the password prompt on its own picture, and never presses Escape. That morning it would have refused and sent me one message. That night we put housesitter’s own sitter on the KVM. The first restart we watched: it refused the FileVault screen, because it had never seen it, so we taught it that screen once, and its one supervised try reached the desktop. After that we only pressed Restart. It saw the boot, recognised FileVault and typed once, and on housesitter 0.2 the desktop was back 70 seconds after Restart, with nobody touching it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-16 py-12 md:py-16 border-t border-canopy/20 bg-deep/40" aria-labelledby="doctor-heading">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto">
      <h2 id="doctor-heading" class="heading-section"><code class="heading-code">housesitter doctor</code></h2>
      <figure class="mt-8">
        <div class="product-media media-shot shot-dark-only"><img src="/images/workshop/housesitter-doctor.webp" width="1800" height="920" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="housesitter doctor mini --private, a real run at 00:34 on 9 November against our mini and its Comet, with the gradient pixel wordmark. The picture is hidden; it reads as a desktop from a decoded frame, the taught prompt not on the picture, the clock moving, and it answers: the Mac's own heartbeat is 0.8 minutes old. The Mac is up at its desktop, for at least 1 hour 37 minutes (since 22:57). The KVM is a GL.iNet Comet on kvmd 4.82 over the LAN, keyboard online, HDMI 2560 by 1440 read by the sitter with no viewer open. The password is verified; it has been taught FileVault, the lock screen and the list of users; the lock screen is unlocked when an agent asks. Budgets: 2 of 2 tries left before it stops, 3 of 3 this hour; no plug, so a frozen Mac is reported, never cycled. Last incident 22:56: a restart's FileVault, unlocked by the sitter, nobody woken. Recent, newest first: 23:30, a TEST alert, posted and emailed; 23:05, a TEST alert; 22:56, the FileVault unlock of the hands-off restart. Alerts go to ntfy and by email, the address shortened to its first letter; nothing outside watches the KVM itself, an open dot. One thing to do: add a dead man's switch for the KVM." /></div>
        <figcaption class="night-caption"><span class="caption-note">Real run</span> <span>The real housesitter doctor of our mini and its Comet at 00:34 on 9 November, an hour and a half after the 70-second restart, drawn with --private so my desktop and my address stay out. Its last incident is the restart it unlocked at 22:56, by itself; it reads the Mac's own heartbeat (its age, never where it lives). The two open dots are true: no plug yet, and nothing outside watches the KVM itself.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="mt-8">
        <div class="product-media media-shot shot-dark-only"><img src="/images/workshop/housesitter-look.webp" width="1800" height="711" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="housesitter look on a real frame of our lock screen: a pixel glimpse of the Golden Gate wallpaper and the avatar. It sees the lock screen it was taught, at 100 percent. May type: only when you ask, over SSH; a lock screen is your lock. Pressed nothing, typed nothing, changed nothing." /></div>
        <figcaption class="night-caption"><span class="caption-note">Real frame</span> <span>housesitter look on our lock screen: the glimpse, and what the sitter makes of it.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="mt-8 text-lg text-mist leading-relaxed">Everything the sitter knows on one screen, with the command for anything missing. The picture is 40 by 22 pixels of the KVM’s own, too small to read anything on.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-16 py-12 md:py-16 border-t border-canopy/20" aria-labelledby="replay-heading">
    <h2 id="replay-heading" class="heading-section max-w-6xl mx-auto">The day, <span class="text-scale">replayed</span></h2>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto replay mt-6">
      <div>
        <p class="text-lg text-mist leading-relaxed"><code class="model-code">housesitter demo</code> replays that Sunday in your terminal: real frames from our KVM, read again just now by the rules the sitter runs, then that day’s two restarts. In the morning’s frame there’s no password field, so it refuses and tells you. In the evening our watchdog, on the same rules, needs teaching once; that night housesitter 0.2 unlocks a restart hands-off, in 70 seconds.</p>
        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-2">
          <pre id="housesitter-demo-2" class="command">{demo}</pre>
          <button type="button" data-copy-target="housesitter-demo-2" class="btn-ghost !py-2 text-sm"><span data-copy-label>Copy</span></button>
        </div>

      </div>
      <div class="product-media media-shot shot-dark-only replay-shot"><img src="/images/workshop/housesitter-evening.webp" width="1694" height="2407" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="housesitter demo: seven pixel glimpses of real frames down the left, each with what the sitter sees and does. 10:32, no password field: it won’t type and tells you once. 17:59, a desktop, not shown. 18:07, the taught prompt at 100 and 93 percent. 18:15, asked once, it typed and the desktop followed, not shown. 18:31 to 18:32:56, our watchdog’s first restart test: taught once, typed once, the desktop 1 min 44 s after the restart. 22:55:55 to 22:57:06, housesitter 0.2: Restart pressed by our agent, the boot, FileVault, the prompt it was taught steady for 6 looks, typed once by itself, the desktop up 70 s after Restart with nobody touching it." /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-16 py-12 md:py-16 border-t border-canopy/20 bg-deep/40" aria-labelledby="night-heading">
    <h2 id="night-heading" class="heading-section max-w-6xl mx-auto">A bad <span class="text-scale">night</span></h2>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto mt-8">
      <figure class="min-w-0 night-clip">
        <WorkshopVideo src="/images/workshop/housesitter-night.mp4" poster="/images/workshop/housesitter-night-poster.webp" width={1280} height={840} alt="A terminal recording of housesitter demo night, all fakes. 03:12 a kernel panic; the sitter sees FileVault and types; back by itself 4 minutes after it went down. 04:31 the Mac freezes; at 04:36 the clock hasn't moved in 5 minutes and nothing answers; three more looks over two minutes; 04:38 the smart plug goes off for 10 seconds; back by itself 12 minutes after. 05:41 another panic after the password was changed; two tries fail; at 05:51 one message to your phone. 3 incidents overnight: 2 fixed by itself, 1 needed you, and it said so once." />
        <figcaption class="night-caption"><span class="caption-note">Rehearsal</span> <span>Recorded in CI from housesitter demo night: a pretend Mac and KVM, run by the real rules and client.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="night-grid mt-10">
        <div class="min-w-0">
        <p class="text-lg text-mist leading-relaxed"><code class="model-code">housesitter demo night</code> rehearses a bad night, all fakes, and each incident tests one rule. A panic: it types at FileVault, once. A freeze: three more looks before the plug goes off. A password someone changed: two tries, then one message, and never a third.</p></div>
        <div class="min-w-0">
        <figure>
          <div class="note-card" role="img" aria-label="An email in HEY: TEST: mini is stuck at FileVault. TEST (housesitter alert-test; nothing is wrong). Seen by the housesitter on its KVM. Stuck because: at the FileVault screen for 10+ min and the sitter has not unlocked it. Look: housesitter status mini.">
            <p class="note-app">HEY Imbox · from ntfy.sh <span>23:30</span></p>
            <p class="note-title">🏠 TEST: mini is stuck at FileVault</p>
            <p class="note-body">TEST (housesitter alert-test; nothing is wrong). Seen by the housesitter on its KVM.</p>
            <p class="note-body">Stuck because: at the FileVault screen for 10+ min and the sitter has not unlocked it. Look: housesitter status mini.</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption class="night-caption"><span class="caption-note">Real run</span> <span>The TEST alert our sitter sent on 8 November, word for word from ntfy’s record, as it landed in our HEY Imbox 2 seconds later. Drawn as a card; no real refusal has happened yet.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-16 py-12 md:py-16 border-t border-canopy/20" aria-labelledby="does-heading">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto">
      <h2 id="does-heading" class="heading-section">What it <span class="text-scale">does</span></h2>
      <ul class="does">
        {does.map(([name, body]) => (
          <li><h3 class="does-name">{name}</h3><p>{body}</p></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-8 text-lg text-mist leading-relaxed">Your agents get the same eyes and hands: screenshots from before login, and click, type and key on the desktop, over your tailnet or LAN, in Anthropic’s computer-use actions as-is.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-16 py-12 md:py-16 border-t border-canopy/20 bg-deep/40" aria-labelledby="why-heading">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto">
      <h2 id="why-heading" class="heading-section">Why not <span class="text-scale">just…</span></h2>
      <ul class="does">
        {whyNot.map(([name, body]) => (
          <li><h3 class="does-name">{name}</h3><p>{body}</p></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="setup" class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-16 py-12 md:py-16 border-t border-canopy/20" aria-labelledby="setup-heading">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto">
      <h2 id="setup-heading" class="heading-section">Three cables, <span class="text-scale">then one restart</span></h2>
      <ol class="cables">
        {cables.map(([name, body], i) => (
          <li><span class="cable-n">{i + 1}</span><p><span class="text-bone">{name}</span> {body}</p></li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-8 text-lg text-mist leading-relaxed">Then, on the agent Mac, with Node 20 or later:</p>
      <pre id="housesitter-install" class="install"><code>npm install -g github:ourostack/housesitter
housesitter secret studio mac-password
housesitter adopt studio --address 192.168.1.50 --this-mac --notify ntfy --heartbeat &lt;healthchecks.io URL&gt;</code></pre>
      <div class="mt-3"><button type="button" data-copy-target="housesitter-install" class="btn-ghost !py-2 text-sm"><span data-copy-label>Copy</span></button></div>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-mist leading-relaxed"><code class="model-code">adopt</code> sets the KVM’s admin password, turns its API on, checks the picture and the keyboard, installs the sitter, seals the Mac’s password on the KVM, and makes a private ntfy topic for your phone, where the sitter says hello before it finishes (add <code class="model-code">--email</code> and an ntfy.sh account token to get each message as an email too); the heartbeat tells you if the KVM itself goes quiet. Then, once, while you watch: arm one try with <code class="model-code">housesitter unlock --trial</code> and restart. At the FileVault screen it waits, because it hasn’t seen that screen before; teach it with <code class="model-code">housesitter learn-prompt --filevault</code> and it types its one try within seconds. If that try reaches the desktop, it may type by itself from then on.</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-mist leading-relaxed">On the Mac, once: <code class="model-code">sudo pmset -a autorestart 1 displaysleep 0 sleep 0</code>, and start your agents at login, so they come back when the Mac does. Built against the GL.iNet Comet PoE, about $100; a Shelly plug for a frozen Mac is about $25 more. At home the KVM’s SSH and HTTPS are unfiltered, so keep it behind your router; elsewhere, <code class="model-code">--location away</code> lets in only your tailnet.</p>
      <p class="mt-6 product-links"><a href={`${repo}#three-cables-then-one-restart`} class={link}>The full setup &#8599;</a> <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/hardware.md`} class={link}>What to buy &#8599;</a> <a href={repo} class={link}>housesitter on GitHub &#8599;</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-16 py-12 md:py-16 border-t border-canopy/20 bg-deep/40" aria-labelledby="safe-heading">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto">
      <h2 id="safe-heading" class="heading-section">Careful with <span class="text-scale">the password</span></h2>
      <ul class="safe">
        {safe.map((line) => <li>{line}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-6 product-links"><a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/security.md`} class={link}>The threat model &#8599;</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-16 py-12 md:py-16 pb-20 border-t border-canopy/20" aria-labelledby="verified-heading">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto">
      <h2 id="verified-heading" class="heading-section">What’s <span class="text-scale">verified</span></h2>
      <ul class="does">
        {verified.map(([name, body]) => (
          <li><h3 class="does-name">{name}</h3><p>{body}</p></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-6 product-links"><a href={`${repo}#whats-verified`} class={link}>Part by part &#8599;</a> <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/docs/proof/2026-11-09-v0.2`} class={link}>The restart’s evidence, and what it can’t show &#8599;</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .command { margin: 0; padding: 0.6rem 1rem; white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: anywhere; border-radius: 0.75rem; background: var(--color-deep); border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-canopy) 60%, transparent); color: var(--color-bone); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  .night { display: grid; gap: 1.25rem; margin-top: 2rem; }
  .night-step { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 4.5rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1rem; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 1rem; background: var(--color-deep); border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-canopy) 60%, transparent); }
  @media (min-width: 768px) { .night-step { grid-template-columns: 6rem minmax(0, 1fr); padding: 1.5rem 1.75rem; } }
  .night-at { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 2rem; line-height: 1; color: var(--color-bone); }
  @media (min-width: 768px) { .night-at { font-size: 2.5rem; } }
  .night-body { display: grid; gap: 0.5rem; color: var(--color-mist); font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  .night-what { color: var(--color-bone); }
  .night-back { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.875rem; letter-spacing: 0.02em; color: var(--color-scale); }
  .night-you .night-back { color: var(--color-fang); }
  .night-caption { display: block; margin-top: var(--caption-gap, 1rem); font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-mist); }
  .restart { display: grid; gap: 1.5rem; margin-top: 2rem; align-items: center; }
  @media (min-width: 1024px) { .restart { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 3fr) minmax(0, 2fr); gap: 2.5rem; } }
  .restart-clip :global(.workshop-video) { width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 1rem; display: block; }
  /* The clip's wrapper is height: 100% for the cover-cropped clips elsewhere; here, a grid item, that resolved to the
     whole figure and pushed the caption out below the hero. */
  .restart-clip { align-self: start; }
  .restart-clip :global(.clip-toggle) { top: 0.6rem; bottom: auto; }
